> Technically unrelated, but probably still important for some people: GNU
> screen uses ^a as command key by default. That's probably one of the
> reasons why I never used it in Nethack. (Of course, it is possible to
> still enter ^a inside screen, you just have to press ^a a. Thinking
> about it, I use that shortcut in bash quite a lot. But it is a little
> annoying.)

Nethack and screen probably chose it by "Let's see; here's a command
people will want to use very frequently/repeatedly. What's the most
accessible place?" The key next to the traditional Control key was a
good answer ...

The use of ^A in Emacs (and bash, and many other programs) to mean "go
to the beginning of the line" is less problematic.  I use it even when
I'm in screen: my fingers automatically translate it into ^A a.

But I don't run Nethack inside screen, so I personally have no issues
there.  If I did, I think I would remap screen to use something else
for that particular session ... assuming it's possible.

